#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# guard-employer-leak (QUM-872, self-checking since QUM-1156)
#
# Scans for forbidden employer/cloud content and blocks a commit that introduces
# it. The repo is PUBLIC: the employer name and corporate cloud identifiers
# (subscription/tenant IDs, resource-group/storage-account names, tag values)
# must never land in the tracked tree.
#
# Two modes:
#   (default) staged  — scans only ADDED lines in the staged diff. This is the
#                       phased "new-additions-only" guard: it runs on every
#                       commit via scripts/pre-commit and does NOT re-flag terms
#                       already present in the tree (removed by QUM-873).
#   --all             — scans the whole tracked tree. LIVE: the Makefile
#                       `leak-scan` target invokes it unconditionally and
#                       `validate` depends on `leak-scan`, so it runs on every
#                       validate. (It was gated off while pre-existing
#                       occurrences were being scrubbed; that scrub landed with
#                       QUM-873 and the gate is gone.)
#   --require-list    — opt-in: treat an absent list as a failure (see EXIT
#                       CODES / the no-list fail-open below).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXIT CODES  (QUM-1156 — code 3 must NEVER collapse into code 0)
#   0  clean:  the scan ran, every required probe class fired, nothing matched.
#   1  violation: forbidden content found. The scan measured what it claims to.
#   2  usage:  bad argument, or an unknown probe class / debug mechanism.
#   3  self-check failed: this run did NOT measure what it claims to measure —
#      a terms-list shortfall, a probe class whose control did not fire, a
#      sub-step that errored, or --require-list with no list.
#
#   Precedence: 3 BEATS 1. If the self-checks did not fire, the violation list
#   is incomplete by construction, so reporting 1 would assert a completeness
#   this run cannot back. Violations found are still printed alongside the 3.
#   An exit 3 is a FAILED scan, never a clean one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THIS SCRIPT CHECKS ITSELF (QUM-1156)
#
# The previous version was silent on success and silently skipped individually
# malformed list entries: a 7-entry list of which 3 were malformed loaded 4
# terms and produced output byte-identical to a list where all 7 loaded. One
# working term is perfectly consistent with six sitting unloaded, so "did
# anything match" cannot detect it. Hence:
#
#   * a COUNT, not a boolean — terms loaded must equal the number of non-blank,
#     non-comment lines in the list, and a shortfall is fatal (naming line
#     NUMBERS only: a malformed entry may still contain a real term);
#   * IN-RUN controls — each probe class scans a synthetic, known-positive
#     subject this invocation and must report it. A control that ran in another
#     process proves nothing about this one;
#   * both LEGS — pos=1 proves the probe is aimed at the right subject, neg=0
#     proves it can still discriminate. A probe that matches everything passes
#     the positive leg forever;
#   * an OBSERVABLE report — the tool prints its own denominator on success, so
#     a clean run is distinguishable from a vacuous one by READING it. Every
#     other remedy requires the caller to build a probe, and probes are what
#     keep failing.
#
# The controls scan a subject this script supplies, never the real diff or the
# real tree: deriving them from the scan subject would make them vacuous
# exactly when the subject is empty, which is when a vacuous scan is most
# dangerous. Control terms are PLACEHOLDERS, so control output is safe to print.
#
# PROBE CLASSES are scan-path × match-type, and the required set is scoped to
# the mode: a control exercising a code path this invocation did not take is not
# evidence about this invocation.
#   always:  list-parse
#   staged:  diff-linewise-{ci,exact}, diff-dewrapped-{ci,exact}
#   --all:   tree-linewise-{ci,exact}, tree-dewrapped-{ci,exact}
#
# WHAT IT STILL CANNOT SEE (reported on every run, so an absence in the output
# is not read as an absence in the tree):
#   * binary files — git grep -I skips them and the de-wrap pass reads the same
#     text-file set. A terraform plan is a zip archive. Out of scope (QUM-1156);
#   * untracked and gitignored paths;
#   * in staged mode: unstaged changes and the pre-existing tree;
#   * a term wrapped across a line break WITH indentation — the de-wrap joins
#     lines but does not strip intra-line whitespace, because doing so would
#     manufacture matches ("foo bar" + "baz" -> "barbaz") on a gate that blocks
#     every commit;
#   * in staged mode, a term split across one ADDED line and one pre-existing
#     line. Catching it would re-flag grandfathered content and break the
#     new-additions-only phasing. `--all` catches it.
#
# The forbidden terms live in a GITIGNORED list — the public repo must never
# contain the list of things it is hiding. A committed scripts/forbidden-terms.example
# documents the format with placeholders only. This guard itself contains NO
# forbidden term.
#
# List resolution (first match wins):
#   1. $SPRAWL_FORBIDDEN_TERMS_FILE  (explicit override; the test/CI seam)
#   2. <main-checkout>/.sprawl/hygiene/forbidden-terms, located via
#      `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` so it resolves to the single canonical
#      list from ANY worktree, regardless of how this guard was installed.
# When the list is ABSENT the guard is a no-op (exit 0), so it is safe to install
# in any repository — a foreign repo simply has nothing to scan. That fail-open
# is deliberate and it STAYS; what changed in QUM-1156 is that it is no longer
# silent (`terms loaded: 0/0 — no list found, scanning nothing`). Pass
# --require-list for the opt-in hard requirement; inverting the default is a
# repo-wide policy question, not a change to this script.
#
# List format, one term per line:  category:matchtype:term
#   matchtype ci    = case-insensitive substring (use for the employer name)
#   matchtype exact = case-sensitive substring   (use for GUIDs / IDs / names)
# Lines that are blank or start with '#' (after optional leading whitespace) are
# skipped and are not counted. Every other line MUST be a well-formed
# category:matchtype:term with a non-empty category and a term carrying no
# leading/trailing whitespace — anything else is fatal (exit 3). A whitespace-
# padded term is rejected rather than trimmed: trimming would silently change
# matching semantics for a real secret, and a whitespace-ONLY term would become
# a substring match on blanks, flagging every line in the tree. An empty or
# miscased matchtype is NOT malformed — it falls back to the broader
# case-insensitive match on purpose (see match_line).
# Violation messages report the category + file:line ONLY — never the term
# itself, so the guard's own output can never leak a secret.
#
# DEBUG SEAMS (test-only, safe by construction — each can only force exit 3 or
# exit 2; none can suppress a real scan's verdict or turn a violation into a
# pass, because a failed self-check outranks a violation):
#   SPRAWL_LEAK_GUARD_DEBUG_SUPPRESS=<class>     drop that class's positive leg
#   SPRAWL_LEAK_GUARD_DEBUG_NEGLEAK=<class>      plant the control term in that
#                                                class's NEGATIVE subject
#   SPRAWL_LEAK_GUARD_DEBUG_BREAK=linewise|dewrap  break the REAL matcher, so
#                                                the control must notice — the
#                                                only thing proving a control
#                                                sits downstream of the code it
#                                                certifies rather than beside it
#
# Invoked by scripts/pre-commit right after guard-main-commit and BEFORE the
# GIT_* unset, so it runs in the committing worktree's git context.
set -euo pipefail

# --- exit codes -------------------------------------------------------------
readonly EXIT_CLEAN=0
readonly EXIT_VIOLATION=1
readonly EXIT_USAGE=2
readonly EXIT_SELFCHECK=3

# --- control placeholders (never real terms; safe to print) -----------------
readonly CONTROL_CI="SPRAWL-LEAKPROBE-CONTROL-CI"
readonly CONTROL_EXACT="SPRAWL-LEAKPROBE-CONTROL-EXACT"

readonly KNOWN_CLASSES=(
	list-parse
	diff-linewise-ci diff-linewise-exact diff-dewrapped-ci diff-dewrapped-exact
	tree-linewise-ci tree-linewise-exact tree-dewrapped-ci tree-dewrapped-exact
)
readonly KNOWN_BREAKS=(linewise dewrap)

usage() {
	echo "usage: guard-employer-leak [--all] [--require-list]" >&2
	exit "$EXIT_USAGE"
}

# die_selfcheck <message...> — exit 3. Used for every "this run did not measure"
# condition, so none of them can reach the exit-0 path.
die_selfcheck() {
	{
		echo "ERROR: guard-employer-leak self-check FAILED — this run did not measure what a"
		echo "clean verdict would claim (QUM-1156). Exit $EXIT_SELFCHECK is NOT a clean scan."
		echo
		printf '%s\n' "$@"
	} >&2
	exit "$EXIT_SELFCHECK"
}

in_list() { # in_list <needle> <haystack...>
	local needle="$1" i
	shift
	for i in "$@"; do [[ "$i" == "$needle" ]] && return 0; done
	return 1
}

# --- arguments --------------------------------------------------------------
mode="staged"
require_list=0
for arg in "$@"; do
	case "$arg" in
	--all) mode="all" ;;
	--require-list) require_list=1 ;;
	*) usage ;;
	esac
done

DEBUG_SUPPRESS="${SPRAWL_LEAK_GUARD_DEBUG_SUPPRESS:-}"
DEBUG_NEGLEAK="${SPRAWL_LEAK_GUARD_DEBUG_NEGLEAK:-}"
DEBUG_BREAK="${SPRAWL_LEAK_GUARD_DEBUG_BREAK:-}"
# An unknown class/mechanism is a USAGE error, not a self-check failure. Without
# this the seams would be satisfiable by echoing back whatever they were handed,
# and the class registry would not have to exist.
for dbg in "$DEBUG_SUPPRESS" "$DEBUG_NEGLEAK"; do
	if [[ -n "$dbg" ]] && ! in_list "$dbg" "${KNOWN_CLASSES[@]}"; then
		echo "guard-employer-leak: unknown probe class '$dbg'" >&2
		echo "known classes: ${KNOWN_CLASSES[*]}" >&2
		exit "$EXIT_USAGE"
	fi
done
if [[ -n "$DEBUG_BREAK" ]] && ! in_list "$DEBUG_BREAK" "${KNOWN_BREAKS[@]}"; then
	echo "guard-employer-leak: unknown debug mechanism '$DEBUG_BREAK'" >&2
	echo "known mechanisms: ${KNOWN_BREAKS[*]}" >&2
	exit "$EXIT_USAGE"
fi

for tool in awk git; do
	command -v "$tool" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
		die_selfcheck "  required tool '$tool' is not installed, so the scan could not run."
done

# Scratch dir for control fixtures and the awk terms file. 0700 via umask: the
# terms file holds the REAL forbidden terms.
umask 077
SCRATCH="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/leakguard.XXXXXX")"
[[ -n "$SCRATCH" && -d "$SCRATCH" ]] ||
	die_selfcheck "  could not create a scratch dir; the in-run controls could not be planted."
# shellcheck disable=SC2064 # expand SCRATCH now: it must not be re-resolved later
trap "rm -rf -- '$SCRATCH'" EXIT

# --- list resolution --------------------------------------------------------
list="${SPRAWL_FORBIDDEN_TERMS_FILE:-}"
if [[ -z "$list" ]]; then
	# `--git-common-dir` (git 2.5+) resolves to the MAIN checkout's .git from any
	# worktree. Avoid `--path-format=absolute` (git 2.31+) — on older git it
	# would fail and leave the list empty, silently no-op'ing the guard even when
	# a list exists (a fail-open). Absolutize in the shell instead: the value may
	# be relative to the current dir (a commit can run from a worktree subdir).
	common="$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null || true)"
	if [[ -n "$common" ]] && common="$(cd "$common" 2>/dev/null && pwd)"; then
		list="$common/../.sprawl/hygiene/forbidden-terms"
	fi
fi

# --- term loading -----------------------------------------------------------
# Sets cats/types/terms and the AC1 accounting: LOAD_TOTAL (non-blank,
# non-comment lines = the denominator), LOAD_OK (the numerator) and LOAD_BAD
# (physical line numbers of the difference — NUMBERS only, never contents).
cats=()
types=()
terms=()
LOAD_TOTAL=0
LOAD_OK=0
LOAD_BAD=""

load_terms() {
	local file="$1" raw line lineno=0 cat rest mtype term
	cats=()
	types=()
	terms=()
	LOAD_TOTAL=0
	LOAD_OK=0
	LOAD_BAD=""
	while IFS= read -r raw || [[ -n "$raw" ]]; do
		lineno=$((lineno + 1))
		line="${raw%$'\r'}" # tolerate CRLF list files
		# Skip blank / whitespace-only and comment lines. Not counted either way.
		[[ "$line" =~ ^[[:space:]]*$ ]] && continue
		[[ "$line" =~ ^[[:space:]]*# ]] && continue
		LOAD_TOTAL=$((LOAD_TOTAL + 1))
		cat="${line%%:*}"
		rest="${line#*:}"
		mtype="${rest%%:*}"
		mtype="${mtype,,}" # normalize: EXACT/Ci/… → lowercase (fail-safe below)
		term="${rest#*:}"
		# Well-formed = category:matchtype:term, non-empty category, non-empty
		# term with no surrounding whitespace. An empty/miscased MATCHTYPE is
		# deliberately allowed through (match_line broadens it).
		if [[ "$line" == "$cat" ]] ||
			[[ "$rest" == "$mtype" ]] ||
			[[ -z "$cat" ]] ||
			[[ -z "$term" ]] ||
			[[ "$term" != "${term#[[:space:]]}" ]] ||
			[[ "$term" != "${term%[[:space:]]}" ]]; then
			LOAD_BAD="${LOAD_BAD:+$LOAD_BAD, }$lineno"
			continue
		fi
		cats+=("$cat")
		types+=("$mtype")
		terms+=("$term")
		LOAD_OK=$((LOAD_OK + 1))
	done <"$file"
}

# --- matchers ---------------------------------------------------------------
# match_line <content> — echoes the matching category (empty if none matched).
match_line() {
	local content="$1" lc i
	# The BREAK seam severs the real matcher so the linewise controls have to
	# notice. It can only ever force exit 3 (a class reporting pos=0).
	[[ "$DEBUG_BREAK" == "linewise" ]] && return 0
	lc="${content,,}"
	for i in "${!terms[@]}"; do
		# Only an explicit "exact" is case-sensitive; anything else (ci, a typo,
		# a miscased value) falls back to the BROADER case-insensitive match so a
		# malformed list entry cannot silently let a lowercase leak slip through.
		if [[ "${types[$i]}" == "exact" ]]; then
			[[ "$content" == *"${terms[$i]}"* ]] && {
				printf '%s' "${cats[$i]}"
				return 0
			}
		else
			[[ "$lc" == *"${terms[$i],,}"* ]] && {
				printf '%s' "${cats[$i]}"
				return 0
			}
		fi
	done
	return 0
}

violations=""
linewise_hits="" # "|file:category|" markers, for de-wrap dedupe

# add_wrapped <file> <category> — record a de-wrapped hit unless the line-wise
# pass already reported that (file, category). A de-wrapped hit carries NO line
# number: the de-wrapped stream has no lines, and reconstructing one would be a
# guess presented as a fact.
add_wrapped() {
	[[ "$linewise_hits" == *"|$1:$2|"* ]] && return 0
	violations+="  $1: $2 (wrapped match)"$'\n'
}

# scan_diff — reads a unified diff (--unified=0) on stdin.
#
# The de-wrap buffer is flushed at every hunk header and every file header,
# because under --unified=0 the added lines of two hunks are NOT adjacent in the
# new file. Joining across a hunk boundary would manufacture a term that exists
# nowhere in the tree — a false alarm on a gate that blocks every commit.
scan_diff() {
	local dl file="" lineno=0 hs m added jfile="" joined=""

	flush_join() {
		local cat
		[[ "$DEBUG_BREAK" == "dewrap" ]] && {
			joined=""
			jfile=""
			return 0
		}
		if [[ -n "$joined" && -n "$jfile" ]]; then
			cat="$(match_line "$joined")"
			[[ -n "$cat" ]] && add_wrapped "$jfile" "$cat"
		fi
		joined=""
		jfile=""
	}

	while IFS= read -r dl; do
		case "$dl" in
		"+++ b/"*)
			flush_join
			file="${dl#+++ b/}"
			;;
		"+++ "*)
			flush_join
			file="" # e.g. +++ /dev/null (deletion)
			;;
		"@@ "*)
			flush_join
			# @@ -a,b +c,d @@ -> new-file start line is c.
			hs="${dl#*+}"
			hs="${hs%% *}"
			lineno="${hs%%,*}"
			;;
		"+"*)
			if [[ -n "$file" ]]; then
				added="${dl#+}"
				m="$(match_line "$added")"
				if [[ -n "$m" ]]; then
					violations+="  $file:$lineno: $m"$'\n'
					linewise_hits+="|$file:$m|"
				fi
				# De-wrap: drop the CR (else a CRLF file can never wrap) and the
				# newline, by plain concatenation.
				jfile="$file"
				joined+="${added%$'\r'}"
			fi
			lineno=$((lineno + 1))
			;;
		*) ;; # context/removed/header lines don't advance the new-file counter
		esac
	done
	flush_join
}

# scan_tree_linewise — one `git grep` per term (C-fast) instead of the old bash
# loop that read every tracked file line-by-line and forked a subshell per line
# (O(files×lines×terms)) — that timed out over the full tree (QUM-873).
#   -F fixed string (the term may contain regex metachars or ':')
#   -I skip binary files (their NULs would break the field split anyway)
#   -n line numbers
#   -z NUL-delimit the filename and line-number fields, so a path or a matching
#      line containing ':' can't be misparsed — and we read only the filename +
#      line number, never the content (the term is never printed).
# Record layout per match: filename\0lineno\0content\n
#
# GREP_EXTRA lets the control run the SAME code path with --no-index against its
# scratch fixture, so the control certifies this function rather than a copy.
GREP_EXTRA=()
scan_tree_linewise() {
	local i rc f n out
	[[ "$DEBUG_BREAK" == "linewise" ]] && return 0
	out="$SCRATCH/grep.out"
	for i in "${!terms[@]}"; do
		local gopts=(--no-color -I -n -z -F "${GREP_EXTRA[@]+"${GREP_EXTRA[@]}"}")
		[[ "${types[$i]}" != "exact" ]] && gopts+=(-i)
		rc=0
		git grep "${gopts[@]}" -e "${terms[$i]}" >"$out" || rc=$?
		# rc 0 = matched, 1 = no match, >1 = git FAILED. Swallowing >1 as "no
		# match" is the exact false-green this script exists to prevent.
		if ((rc > 1)); then
			die_selfcheck "  git grep exited $rc — the whole-tree line-wise scan did not measure."
		fi
		while IFS= read -r -d '' f && IFS= read -r -d '' n && IFS= read -r _; do
			violations+="  $f:$n: ${cats[$i]}"$'\n'
			linewise_hits+="|$f:${cats[$i]}|"
		done <"$out"
	done
}

# scan_tree_dewrapped — reads a NUL-delimited file list on stdin and searches a
# newline-stripped rendering of each file.
#
# `git grep` structurally cannot do this (it is line-oriented), and a pure-bash
# reader is the O(files×lines×terms) shape QUM-873 already killed: measured over
# this tree it did not finish in 2 minutes. One awk process does it in ~0.5s.
#
# The terms go to awk in a FILE, not argv, because argv is world-readable via
# `ps`. awk emits filename + term INDEX, never the term, so the mapping back to
# a category happens here and the term is never printed.
#
# Memory is O(longest term), not O(file): a match ending inside the current line
# can start at most len(longest term)-1 characters earlier, so carrying that
# many characters forward is provably sufficient.
scan_tree_dewrapped() {
	local tf f idx rc=0
	if [[ "$DEBUG_BREAK" == "dewrap" ]]; then
		cat >/dev/null
		return 0
	fi
	((${#terms[@]} > 0)) || {
		cat >/dev/null
		return 0
	}

	tf="$SCRATCH/awk-terms"
	: >"$tf"
	for idx in "${!terms[@]}"; do
		# Paired lines: matchtype, then term. Terms cannot contain a newline by
		# construction (the list is one term per line), so this is unambiguous.
		printf '%s\n%s\n' "${types[$idx]}" "${terms[$idx]}" >>"$tf"
	done

	# awk indexes from 1; the bash arrays from 0.
	while IFS= read -r -d '' f && IFS= read -r -d '' idx; do
		add_wrapped "$f" "${cats[$((idx - 1))]}"
	done < <(xargs -0 -r awk -v TF="$tf" '
	function hit(i) {
		if (!(FILENAME SUBSEP i in seen)) {
			seen[FILENAME SUBSEP i] = 1
			printf "%s%c%d%c", FILENAME, 0, i, 0
		}
	}
	BEGIN {
		while ((getline l < TF) > 0) {
			n++
			if (n % 2 == 1) { mt[(n + 1) / 2] = l }
			else {
				k = n / 2
				tm[k] = l; lctm[k] = tolower(l)
				if (length(l) > W) W = length(l)
			}
		}
		NT = int(n / 2)
	}
	FNR == 1 { win = "" }
	{
		line = $0; sub(/\r$/, "", line)
		s = win line; slc = tolower(s)
		for (i = 1; i <= NT; i++) {
			if (mt[i] == "exact") { if (index(s, tm[i]) > 0) hit(i) }
			else { if (index(slc, lctm[i]) > 0) hit(i) }
		}
		L = length(s)
		win = (L >= W ? substr(s, L - W + 2) : s)
	}
	' || rc=$?)
	((rc == 0)) ||
		die_selfcheck "  the de-wrapped pass exited $rc — it did not measure."
}

# --- in-run controls --------------------------------------------------------
# Each control plants a known-positive subject and a known-negative one, runs
# the REAL scan function over them, and reports "class pos neg". Run in a
# subshell with its own cats/types/terms so a control term can never reach the
# real scan's verdict.

plant_control_fixture() { # $1 = dir, $2 = "diff" | "tree"
	local d="$1" kind="$2" cls negfile
	mkdir -p "$d"

	# Positive subjects. The line-wise and de-wrapped positives are SEPARATE
	# files: if the term were present unwrapped in the de-wrap subject, dedupe
	# would suppress the de-wrapped hit and its control could never fire.
	printf 'alpha %s omega\nbeta %s omega\n' "$CONTROL_CI" "$CONTROL_EXACT" >"$d/pos-linewise.txt"
	printf 'alpha %s\n%s omega\n' "${CONTROL_CI:0:20}" "${CONTROL_CI:20}" >"$d/pos-dewrapped-ci.txt"
	printf 'alpha %s\n%s omega\n' "${CONTROL_EXACT:0:20}" "${CONTROL_EXACT:20}" >"$d/pos-dewrapped-exact.txt"

	# Negative subjects, one per class, so a leak planted in one cannot be
	# credited to another.
	for cls in "diff-linewise-ci" "diff-linewise-exact" "diff-dewrapped-ci" "diff-dewrapped-exact" \
		"tree-linewise-ci" "tree-linewise-exact" "tree-dewrapped-ci" "tree-dewrapped-exact"; do
		negfile="$d/neg-$cls.txt"
		printf 'nothing forbidden on this line at all\n' >"$negfile"
		if [[ "$DEBUG_NEGLEAK" == "$cls" ]]; then
			case "$cls" in
			*-dewrapped-ci) printf '%s\n%s\n' "${CONTROL_CI:0:20}" "${CONTROL_CI:20}" >>"$negfile" ;;
			*-dewrapped-exact) printf '%s\n%s\n' "${CONTROL_EXACT:0:20}" "${CONTROL_EXACT:20}" >>"$negfile" ;;
			*-linewise-ci) printf '%s\n' "$CONTROL_CI" >>"$negfile" ;;
			*-linewise-exact) printf '%s\n' "$CONTROL_EXACT" >>"$negfile" ;;
			esac
		fi
	done

	[[ "$kind" == "diff" ]] || return 0
	# Render the same fixture as a --unified=0 diff, so the diff-header and
	# hunk-arithmetic parsing is exercised too — including a rename hunk, which
	# must contribute no added lines.
	{
		printf 'diff --git a/renamed-away.txt b/renamed-here.txt\n'
		printf 'similarity index 100%%\nrename from renamed-away.txt\nrename to renamed-here.txt\n'
		for f in pos-linewise.txt pos-dewrapped-ci.txt pos-dewrapped-exact.txt \
			neg-diff-linewise-ci.txt neg-diff-linewise-exact.txt \
			neg-diff-dewrapped-ci.txt neg-diff-dewrapped-exact.txt; do
			printf 'diff --git a/%s b/%s\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/%s\n' "$f" "$f" "$f"
			printf '@@ -0,0 +1,%d @@\n' "$(wc -l <"$d/$f")"
			sed 's/^/+/' "$d/$f"
		done
	} >"$d/control.diff"
}

# ctl_leg <needle> — 1 if the needle appears in this control run's violations.
ctl_leg() {
	if [[ "$violations" == *"$1"* ]]; then echo 1; else echo 0; fi
}

# emit_class <class> <positive-needle> <negative-needle>
emit_class() {
	local cls="$1" pos neg
	pos="$(ctl_leg "$2")"
	neg="$(ctl_leg "$3")"
	[[ "$DEBUG_SUPPRESS" == "$cls" ]] && pos=0
	printf '%s %s %s\n' "$cls" "$pos" "$neg"
}

run_content_controls() { # $1 = "diff" | "tree"; echoes "class pos neg" lines
	local kind="$1" dir="$SCRATCH/ctl-$1" prefix
	(
		plant_control_fixture "$dir" "$kind"
		cats=(leakprobe-ci leakprobe-exact)
		types=(ci exact)
		terms=("$CONTROL_CI" "$CONTROL_EXACT")
		violations=""
		linewise_hits=""
		if [[ "$kind" == "diff" ]]; then
			prefix="diff"
			scan_diff <"$dir/control.diff"
		else
			prefix="tree"
			cd "$dir"
			GREP_EXTRA=(--no-index)
			scan_tree_linewise
			# Process substitution, NOT a pipe: the right-hand side of a pipe runs
			# in a subshell, so every de-wrapped hit it accumulates would be
			# discarded — the scan would run and its findings evaporate.
			scan_tree_dewrapped < <(printf '%s\0' ./*.txt)
		fi
		emit_class "$prefix-linewise-ci" "pos-linewise.txt:1: leakprobe-ci" "neg-$prefix-linewise-ci.txt"
		emit_class "$prefix-linewise-exact" "pos-linewise.txt:2: leakprobe-exact" "neg-$prefix-linewise-exact.txt"
		emit_class "$prefix-dewrapped-ci" "pos-dewrapped-ci.txt: leakprobe-ci (wrapped match)" "neg-$prefix-dewrapped-ci.txt"
		emit_class "$prefix-dewrapped-exact" "pos-dewrapped-exact.txt: leakprobe-exact (wrapped match)" "neg-$prefix-dewrapped-exact.txt"
	)
}

# The list-parse control: a known list whose shortfall accounting is known, so
# the AC1 arithmetic itself is covered by a control rather than only by tests.
run_list_control() {
	local f="$SCRATCH/ctl-list" pos=0 neg=0
	printf '%s\n' "# comment" "" "x:ci:CONTROLONE" "MALFORMEDCONTROL" "y:exact:CONTROLTWO" >"$f"
	(
		load_terms "$f"
		# Expected: 3 counted lines, 2 loaded, the malformed one on line 4.
		if [[ "$LOAD_TOTAL" == 3 && "$LOAD_OK" == 2 && "$LOAD_BAD" == "4" ]]; then pos=1; fi
		# Negative leg: a list with NO malformed entry must produce no offenders.
		printf '%s\n' "x:ci:CONTROLONE" >"$f.clean"
		load_terms "$f.clean"
		if [[ -n "$LOAD_BAD" || "$LOAD_OK" != 1 ]]; then neg=1; fi
		[[ "$DEBUG_SUPPRESS" == "list-parse" ]] && pos=0
		printf 'list-parse %s %s\n' "$pos" "$neg"
	)
}

# --- run the controls, then the real scan -----------------------------------
ledger=""
ledger+="$(run_list_control)"$'\n'
if [[ "$mode" == "staged" ]]; then
	ledger+="$(run_content_controls diff)"$'\n'
	required=(list-parse diff-linewise-ci diff-linewise-exact diff-dewrapped-ci diff-dewrapped-exact)
else
	ledger+="$(run_content_controls tree)"$'\n'
	required=(list-parse tree-linewise-ci tree-linewise-exact tree-dewrapped-ci tree-dewrapped-exact)
fi

# Evaluate the floor. Per-class, never a sum: a total cannot tell four-fired-once
# from one-fired-four-times, and it is the zero that matters.
floor_failures=""
report_ledger=""
for cls in "${required[@]}"; do
	line="$(printf '%s\n' "$ledger" | awk -v c="$cls" '$1 == c {print; exit}')"
	# A control subshell that died early contributes nothing, and `[ "" -lt 1 ]`
	# both errors AND evaluates false — a floor that cannot fire. Validate the
	# shape before comparing.
	if [[ -z "$line" ]]; then
		floor_failures+="  $cls: MISSING — its control produced no result at all"$'\n'
		continue
	fi
	pos="$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $2}')"
	neg="$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $3}')"
	if [[ ! "$pos" =~ ^[0-9]+$ || ! "$neg" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
		floor_failures+="  $cls: non-numeric control result — the floor could not be evaluated"$'\n'
		continue
	fi
	report_ledger+="  $cls: pos=$pos neg=$neg"$'\n'
	if ((pos < 1)); then
		floor_failures+="  $cls: pos=$pos neg=$neg (want pos>=1 — its positive control did NOT fire)"$'\n'
	elif ((neg != 0)); then
		floor_failures+="  $cls: pos=$pos neg=$neg (want neg=0 — it fired on a clean subject, so it cannot discriminate)"$'\n'
	fi
done

# --- the success report (stdout; violations go to stderr) -------------------
echo "guard-employer-leak: $mode scan (QUM-1156 self-checking; exit 3 = did not measure)"
printf '%s' "$report_ledger"
echo "  control terms (placeholders, never real): $CONTROL_CI $CONTROL_EXACT"

not_examined_staged="  not examined: unstaged changes, untracked files, gitignored paths, binary blobs (-I), and the pre-existing tree (this scan reports NEW additions only)"

report_not_examined() {
	if [[ "$mode" == "staged" ]]; then
		echo "$not_examined_staged"
		return 0
	fi
	local all_files=0 text_files=0 untracked=0 rc=0
	all_files="$(git grep -l -e '' 2>/dev/null | wc -l || true)"
	text_files="$(git grep -I -l -e '' 2>/dev/null | wc -l || true)"
	untracked="$(git ls-files --others --exclude-standard 2>/dev/null | wc -l || true)"
	rc=$((all_files - text_files))
	((rc >= 0)) || rc=0
	echo "  not examined: $rc binary file(s) skipped by -I, $untracked untracked file(s), plus all gitignored paths"
}

# --- no list => documented no-op pass, but no longer silent -----------------
if [[ -z "$list" || ! -f "$list" ]]; then
	echo "  terms loaded: 0/0 — no list found, scanning nothing"
	report_not_examined
	if ((require_list)); then
		die_selfcheck "  --require-list was passed and no forbidden-terms list was found." \
			"  Looked for: ${list:-<unresolved>}"
	fi
	if [[ -n "$floor_failures" ]]; then
		die_selfcheck "  probe classes that did not measure:" "$floor_failures"
	fi
	exit "$EXIT_CLEAN"
fi

load_terms "$list"
echo "  terms loaded: $LOAD_OK/$LOAD_TOTAL"

if ((LOAD_OK != LOAD_TOTAL)); then
	# Fatal, and BEFORE any scanning: a scan run with a partially-loaded list
	# would produce a verdict indistinguishable from a fully-loaded one, which is
	# the dishonest pass this whole change exists to remove.
	die_selfcheck \
		"  terms loaded: $LOAD_OK/$LOAD_TOTAL — the forbidden-terms list did not fully load." \
		"  Malformed entries at lines: $LOAD_BAD" \
		"  (contents withheld on purpose: a malformed entry may still contain a real term)" \
		"" \
		"  Every non-blank, non-comment line must be category:matchtype:term with a" \
		"  non-empty category and a term carrying no leading/trailing whitespace." \
		"" \
		"  Forbidden-terms list: $list"
fi

if [[ "$mode" == "staged" ]]; then
	# core.quotePath=false keeps non-ASCII paths literal (an unquoted "b/…"
	# header), and the explicit a/ b/ prefixes override a user's
	# diff.mnemonicPrefix / diff.noprefix config — without these a header the
	# `+++ b/` arm doesn't recognize would silently skip that file's added
	# lines (a fail-open leak).
	#
	# diff.renames is PINNED rather than assumed (QUM-1156 AC7). This is the
	# false-ALARM direction: under a user's `diff.renames=false` a rename is
	# re-presented as a whole-file add, so every grandfathered line is reported
	# as newly added and a commit that introduced nothing gets blocked.
	scan_diff < <(git -c core.quotePath=false -c diff.renames=true \
		diff --cached --unified=0 --no-color --src-prefix=a/ --dst-prefix=b/)
else
	scan_tree_linewise
	# `-e ''` matches every line, so this lists exactly the tracked TEXT files —
	# the same set git grep -I searched. rc 1 just means "no files".
	# Process substitution, not a pipe — see the note in run_content_controls.
	scan_tree_dewrapped < <(git grep -I -l -z -e '' || true)
fi

report_not_examined

if [[ -n "$violations" ]]; then
	cat >&2 <<EOF
ERROR: forbidden employer/cloud content detected ($mode scan) — QUM-872 guard.

The locations below match a category in the gitignored forbidden-terms list
(the matching term is withheld from this message on purpose). A "(wrapped
match)" entry means the term spans a line break, so it has a file but no
meaningful line number:

$violations
Public-repo hygiene: employer names and corporate cloud identifiers must never
be committed. Move real values to a gitignored path (e.g.
.sprawl/agents/<name>/findings/) and reference them indirectly. See CLAUDE.md
"Public vs Private Repo Hygiene".

Forbidden-terms list: $list
EOF
fi

# Precedence: a failed self-check outranks a violation. The violations above are
# still printed — the operator needs to see them — but the run may not claim the
# list is complete.
if [[ -n "$floor_failures" ]]; then
	die_selfcheck "  probe classes that did not measure:" "$floor_failures"
fi

[[ -n "$violations" ]] && exit "$EXIT_VIOLATION"
exit "$EXIT_CLEAN"
